ing  to find new purpose to their lives  soccer is no longer an option but other  kinds of sport still give them a strong  boost  on to south sudan where four years after  independence power struggles and  conflict remain the order of the day  a man of the cloth is doing his best to  stop that in the village of kuron  even experienced pilots have a hard time  finding the sandy landing strip  the region that bishop parid taban chose  for his peace project is one of south  sudan's most remote  residents of the village of kuran have  been waiting for several hours for the  clergyman's arrival  they say that since the bishop has been  involved here the whole region has  flourished and there has been less  fighting  the bishop says the concept is a simple  one  we bring people to self-reliance food  and so on  and bringing them new ideas especially  now with education vocational training  koran is located in an area that sees  bitter fighting among rival ethnic  groups mostly over access to pasture  land and cattle which are of primary  importance to the region's economy  the civil war that raged almost non-stop  for decades in south sudan brought  thousands of weapons into the country  ever since traditional cattle raids have  turned into violent conflict  bishop tawban finds the situation  unacceptable he has risked his life  countless times in the past few decades  to take part in peace talks and even at  the age of 79 he's still eager to work  hard under often grueling conditions  the bishop is especially proud of the  peace village's vocational training  center which he financed with church  donations from europe he mainly wants to  offer young men alternatives to fighting  and he's not afraid of getting his own  hands dirty carpentry skills are part of  a south sudanese bishop's job  description  when they see a building like that they  say wow  is it made by miracle  is it god who made it they don't know  that this human being who can make this  they think that they cannot do this or  when they see this oh these have been  done by some  european  might be some arabs who have been here  they destroyed because they think that  he's not done by one of them  but now when they learn that they know  that this is the work of their own hand  for a while now the village of quran has  hosted weddings between members of clans  that were previously bitter enemies  mary namuno a geo woman just recently  said i do to joseph lemoy a man from the  toposa tribe  at first my family was not happy that i  should marry a toposa man  but now they accept it  it's easier to cooperate than it is to  fight  that's also the message of a new play  from koran's drama troupe  newlywed mary nomuno has come to watch  along with almost everyone else in the  my village grave here  that's to show that i'm not going to  live here i retire only once in my life  and i'm going to retire here  until then the bishop still wants to  take care of a few things he wants to  build roads so that more people can come  to coron  and he still needs more time to  celebrate the progress that has already  been made  greece has been dominating the headlines  of late  deep in debt its economy in tatters is  there any hope  we met some young entrepreneurs in the  capital athens  it's exam time at athens university  these economic students are nervous of  course not only because of their grades  they're also worried about the situation  in their country  a lot of them fear that they won't be  able to find a job in greece after  they've graduated  to be quite honest i'm thinking of going  abroad  i don't want to leave  actually i'd rather live and work here  i'll try it but if it doesn't work out  i'm ready for anything  many of these students say a lot has to  change in greece that paying taxes  should be normal and civil servants  shouldn't have to be bribed to do their  job  it's not a matter of financial crisis so  it's not about the greek economy it's  about the greek way of thinking it's  about greek  culture and about greek mentality in  general this cannot be fixed with  eurogroup or with the imf or even with  the government we have right now  but others are determined to stay no  matter what the orange grove center is a  place where young greek entrepreneurs  can get together  88 startups now have office space here  although the center has only been up and  running for a year and a half janissaris  is one of them his big olive online  platform offers themed tours of athens a  niche market he previously lived in  sweden and spain but he was determined  to return home  believe it or not it's a country where  you know a private initiative can  actually be very rewarding because  you know you can become more flexible  and take advantage of all this potential  that happens so it was a very  conscious choice i like it here  antones argeros has also found a niche  he's developed software for trying out  clothes online  the program is selling especially well  in the middle east where women aren't  allowed to change their clothes in  publicly accessible areas  the 34 year old says greece is highly  competitive not least because its people  are extremely well educated and highly  trained  if you want to find a developer for  example in the u.s you have to pay him  three and four times what you pay here  in  the same developer so it's far more  cheaper to have all your tech teams here  the orange grove center was the  brainchild of dutch ambassador jan  festek who's celebrating with some of  the 140 people who now work here  unusual times ask for unusual  initiatives and we now have a situation  in which in europe people have very  strong prejudices about each other  there's a lot of negative atmosphere and  we thought let's do something positive  it's party time despite the country's  crisis  and these young entrepreneurs are hoping  that many more of their compatriots will  start their own businesses in greece  our final segment this week takes us  back to germany  world stories visits a clockmaker in the  heart of the black forest who's working  on a masterpiece destined for far away  mecca  a tower clock maker can climb tens of  thousands of stairs during a career the  clocks are usually huge and perched atop  tall towers this one rises 60 meters  above the town  it embodies an unbelievable amount of  knowledge handed down through  generations and it has to be made of  good compatible materials and they're  our company's secret  some of pero turmoiren's 30 employees  make replacements for broken dials some  of them put a gold leaf finish to clock  hands  the company employs clockmakers  mechanics locksmiths and blacksmiths  they all work together to produce things  like the brackets which suspend the  enormous clocks  the blacksmiths make spare parts for  various mechanisms like bell hammers  a new tower clock can cost as much as 40  000 euros  for this major order from mecca the  client had to pay family owned perro  several million euros the hour hand  alone was 17 meters long  the old dials in the company's 150 year  old archive are real treasures  they serve as models for the latest  order and are constantly being copied it  can take four to ten weeks to complete a  tower clock depending on its size  this clock is on its way to israel the  company's founder built the original  model in 1877  now it'll strike the time again in tel  aviv  you

